**Claim:** $f$ is measurable with respect to the product $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$.

**Proof.**

The product space is $\Omega\times\mathbb{N}$ equipped with the product $\sigma$-algebra
$$\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})=\sigma\big(\{A\times B:A\in\mathcal{L}(\Omega),\,B\subseteq\mathbb{N}\}\big),$$
where $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ is the full power set of $\mathbb{N}$ (the $\sigma$-algebra associated to the counting measure).

Since $\mathbb{N}$ is countable, every subset $B\subseteq\mathbb{N}$ is a countable union of singletons, so the product $\sigma$-algebra is generated by the "rectangles" $A\times\{i\}$ with $A\in\mathcal{L}(\Omega)$ and $i\in\mathbb{N}$:
$$\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})=\sigma\big(\{A\times\{i\}:A\in\mathcal{L}(\Omega),\,i\in\mathbb{N}\}\big).$$

To prove $f$ is measurable it suffices to show that for every $c\in\mathbb{R}$ the superlevel set
$$E_c:=\{(x,i)\in\Omega\times\mathbb{N}:f(x,i)>c\}$$
belongs to $\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$.

Decompose $E_c$ along the fibers indexed by $i$:
$$E_c=\bigcup_{i\in\mathbb{N}}\underbrace{\{x\in\Omega:f(x,i)>c\}}_{=:\,A_{i,c}}\times\{i\}.$$

Indeed, $(x,i)\in E_c \iff f(x,i)>c \iff x\in A_{i,c}$, which is exactly the condition for $(x,i)$ to lie in $A_{i,c}\times\{i\}$.

By hypothesis, for each fixed $i\in\mathbb{N}$ the slice $f(\cdot,i):\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$ is $\mathcal{L}(\Omega)$-measurable. Hence each $A_{i,c}=\{x\in\Omega:f(x,i)>c\}$ is a Lebesgue-measurable subset of $\Omega$, i.e. $A_{i,c}\in\mathcal{L}(\Omega)$.

Therefore each $A_{i,c}\times\{i\}$ is a measurable rectangle in $\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$. The union is countable (over $i\in\mathbb{N}$), and a $\sigma$-algebra is closed under countable unions, so
$$E_c=\bigcup_{i\in\mathbb{N}}A_{i,c}\times\{i\}\in\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}).$$

Since $E_c$ is measurable for every $c\in\mathbb{R}$, the function $f$ is $\mathcal{L}(\Omega)\otimes\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$-measurable. $\blacksquare$

**Answer: Yes, $f$ is measurable.**

The key point is that the counting measure on $\mathbb{N}$ uses the full power set as its $\sigma$-algebra, and $\mathbb{N}$ is countable, so the product $\sigma$-algebra is generated by the single-fiber rectangles $A\times\{i\}$. The slice-wise measurability hypothesis gives exactly the measurability of each fiber piece, and a countable union reassembles them into a measurable set.

